The final match of the [[1998 FIFA World Cup]], the 16th edition of the quadrennial [[association football|football]] competition organised by [[FIFA]] for the men's national teams of its member associations, was played at the [[Stade de France]] in [[Paris]], France, on 12 July 1998, and was contested by defending champions [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and hosts [[France national football team|France]]. 

The tournament featured France as the hosts, Brazil as the winners of the previous World Cup and 30 other teams who emerged from the [[1998 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualification tournaments]] organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]]. The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[1998 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]]. En route to the final, Brazil finished first in [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group A|Group A]], with two wins and one defeat, after which they beat [[Chile national football team|Chile]] in the [[round of 16]], [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] in the quarter-finals and the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] in the semi-finals. France finished top of [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]] with three wins, before defeating [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] in the round of 16, [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in the quarter-final, and [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]] in the semi-final. The final took place in front of 80,000 supporters, with an estimated 1.3&nbsp;billion watching on television, and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Said Belqola]] from Morocco.

Before the match, speculation surrounded the fitness of striker [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]], who was initially left out of Brazil's starting line-up, only to be restored to the team before [[Kick-off (association football)|kick-off]]. France took the lead shortly before the half-hour mark, when [[Zinedine Zidane]] outjumped [[Leonardo Araújo|Leonardo]] to connect with a [[Header (association football)|header]] from an in-swinging [[Corner kick|corner]] from the right taken by [[Emmanuel Petit]]. Zidane scored again, with another header from a corner, shortly before half-time to give France a 2–0 lead. Petit then added a third goal in second-half [[injury time]], striking the ball low into the net following a pass by [[Patrick Vieira]], to complete a 3–0 win for France.

France's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|first World Cup title]], as they became the seventh different nation to win the tournament. Ronaldo was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]] as FIFA's outstanding player of the tournament. Following the win, hundreds of thousands of French supporters celebrated in Paris throughout the night, before assembling along the [[Champs-Élysées]] the following day for an [[open-top bus]] tour by the French players. France followed up their victory by winning their next major tournament at [[UEFA Euro 2000]]. Ronaldo's fitness for the match and his initial omission and later reinstatement on Brazil's teamsheet became a subject of ongoing journalistic interest following the match, with analysis continuing more than 20 years later.

==Background==
[[File:1998 - Tricolore (France) (4170715889).jpg|thumb|An example of the [[Adidas Tricolore]] ball used in the match|alt=View of the match ball]]
The [[1998 FIFA World Cup]] was the 16th edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s football competition for men's national teams, held in France between 10 June and 12 July 1998.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite encyclopedia |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828023709/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=28 August 2021 |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=19 August 2021 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World awaits cup draw |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=4 December 1997 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/36829.stm |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029135023/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/36829.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> The finals featured 32 teams for the first time, up from 24 in [[1994 FIFA World Cup|the 1994 World Cup]].<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4uJdBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA58 |page=58 |title=Encyclopedia of the FIFA World Cup |first=Tom |last=Dunmore |publisher=Scarecrow Press |year=2015 |isbn=978-0-81088-743-5}}</ref> Both [[France national football team|France]] and [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] qualified automatically for the tournament – France as hosts and Brazil because they had won the tournament in 1994.<ref name="1998qualifying">{{cite web |title=World Cup 1998 Qualifying |date=12 December 2002 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/98q.html |first1=Karel |last1=Stokkermans |first2=Sergio Henrique |last2=Jarreta |archive-date=1 June 2018 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180601125924/http://rsssf.com/tables/98q.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The remaining 30 spots were decided through [[1998 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] held between March 1996 and November 1997, organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and involving 168 teams.<ref name="1998qualifying"/><ref>{{cite book |title=Historical Dictionary of Soccer |first=Tom |last=Dunmore |edition=illustrated |publisher=Scarecrow Press |year=2011 |page=111 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9j1wbp2t1usC&pg=PA111 |isbn=978-0-81087-188-5 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613171711/https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Historical_Dictionary_of_Soccer/9j1wbp2t1usC?hl=en&pg=PA111 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the finals, the teams were divided into eight groups of four with each team playing each other once in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The two top teams from each group advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knock-out stage]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1998 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 April 2022 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/98f.html |archive-date=29 December 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201229202553/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/98f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The game was played at the [[Stade de France]], in the northern [[Paris]] suburb of [[Saint-Denis, Seine-Saint-Denis|Saint-Denis]], an 80,000-capacity stadium which was purpose-built for the 1998 FIFA World Cup because there were no previously existing venues large enough to accommodate the final.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stadium history |publisher=[[Stade de France]] |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.stadefrance.com/en/stadium/stadium-history |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029141812/https://www.stadefrance.com/en/stadium/stadium-history |url-status=live }}</ref>

Brazil had won the previous World Cup in the United States in 1994, defeating [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] in [[1994 FIFA World Cup final|the final]] after the match had finished 0–0 after extra time, the first ever goalless final.<ref name="Britanica"/><ref name="RSSSF94">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1994 finals |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94full.html |archive-date=19 December 2017 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171219031509/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/94full.html |url-status=live }}</ref> They began the 1998 tournament as favourites with [[Mário Zagallo]], their [[Manager (association football)|manager]], having been involved with all four previous Brazilian wins – as a player in [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]] and [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], as manager in [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]] and assistant manager in 1994.<ref>{{Cite web |work=[[The Guardian]] |title=Brazil haunted by glorious past |last=Walker |first=Michael |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=1 June 1998 |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/260742842/ |url-access=subscription |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029185837/https://www.newspapers.com/image/260742842/ |url-status=live }}</ref> France did not qualify for the 1994 tournament because they were eliminated in [[1994 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 6|their qualifying group]], finishing behind [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]] and [[Bulgaria national football team|Bulgaria]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1994 qualifications |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94qual.html |archive-date=11 May 2011 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110511105448/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/94qual.html |url-status=live }}</ref> However, they had followed this up with a run to the semi-finals at [[UEFA Euro 1996]], in which they were beaten in a penalty shoot-out by the [[Czech Republic national football team|Czech Republic]]. France's midfielder [[Zinedine Zidane]] had been tied for third place in the 1997 [[FIFA World Player of the Year]] contest and was labelled by former Brazilian forward [[Pelé]] as "one of the players to watch" at the 1998 World Cup.<ref name="VancouverSunDeadAim"/> Before the tournament, [[Aimé Jacquet]], the manager of France, told reporters that his team were "here to win the World Cup, nothing less".<ref name="VancouverSunDeadAim">{{cite web |page=F8 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |url-access=subscription |newspaper=[[Vancouver Sun]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/495977924/ |title=French taking dead aim at winning World Cup |access-date=29 October 2021 |date=21 May 1998 |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030082052/https://www.newspapers.com/image/495977924/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The two teams had met twice previously in the World Cup – in the semi-final of the 1958 tournament, which Brazil won 5–2, and the quarter-final [[1986 FIFA World Cup|in 1986]] when France won in a penalty shoot-out after the game finished 1–1 after extra time.<ref name="11v11FraVsBra">{{Cite web |title=France national football team: record v Brazil |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/france/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029150847/https://www.11v11.com/teams/france/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1958 finals |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/58full.html |archive-date=2 February 2015 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150202134755/http://rsssf.com/tables/58full.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1986 finals |date= |access-date=29 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/86full.html |archive-date=1 March 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301052859/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/86full.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Their most recent meeting had been in the opening match of the [[1997 Tournoi de France]], which finished 1–1.<ref name="11v11FraVsBra"/> [[Roberto Carlos]] had scored Brazil's goal in that game with a famous ''Banana Shot'' [[direct free kick|free kick]], curling from a 33.13-metre distance and often considered to be one of the best in the modern game.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/science-environment-11153466|title=Roberto Carlos wonder goal 'no fluke', say physicists|date=10 September 2010|work=BBC News|access-date=13 August 2013|archive-date=12 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230312120239/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/science-environment-11153466|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>[https://youtube.com/watch?v=ffvojZONF_A The Cross Ratio] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240807042909/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ffvojZONF_A |date=7 August 2024 }}, Numberphile, 2018.</ref>

The match ball used at the tournament was the [[Adidas Tricolore]], which was specifically made for the World Cup. The [[Flag of France|tricolour flag]] and [[Gallic rooster]], traditional symbols of France were used as inspiration for the design.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/world-cup-2010/pictures/6670565/Fifa-World-Cup-match-balls-through-time.html?image=24|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120911180746/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/world-cup-2010/pictures/6670565/Fifa-World-Cup-match-balls-through-time.html?image=24|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 September 2012|work=[[Telegraph.co.uk]] |access-date=12 March 2013|title=Fifa World Cup match balls through time |date=27 November 2009}}</ref> More than 20 drafts had been proposed by the Adidas design team before the definitive version was approved.<ref name="fifa">{{cite web |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190830022413/https://es.fifa.com/news/tricolore-asi-llama-nueva-estrella-76133 |archive-date=2019-08-30 |language=es |title=Tricolore – así se llama la nueva estrella |url=https://es.fifa.com/news/tricolore-asi-llama-nueva-estrella-76133 |date=25 December 1997 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=30 October 2021}}</ref>

