Brazil were in [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group A|Group A]] at the World Cup, in which they were joined by [[Morocco national football team|Morocco]], [[Norway national football team|Norway]] and [[Scotland national football team|Scotland]].<ref name="RSSSF98">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1998 finals |date= |access-date=27 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/98full.html |archive-date=28 January 2015 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150128114726/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/98full.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Their first game was the tournament opener at the Stade de France, on 10 June against Scotland.<ref name="BBCBrazilScot">{{Cite web |title=Own goal gives Brazil victory |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=10 June 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/110435.stm |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030084102/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/110435.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil took the lead after four minutes when [[César Sampaio]] received the ball in the [[penalty area]] from a [[Corner kick|corner]] taken by [[Bebeto]], and [[Header (association football)|headed]] the ball past [[Jim Leighton]] in the Scotland goal.<ref name="GuardianBrazilScot">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024077/ |title=Boyd gifts it to ruffled Brazil |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |first=David |last=Lacey |page=29 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=11 June 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231323/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024077/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Scotland equalised through a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] scored by [[John Collins (footballer, born 1968)|John Collins]], after [[Kevin Gallacher]] had been [[Foul (association football)|fouled]] in the penalty area.<ref name="BBCBrazilScot"/> Brazil took the lead again in the second half when a [[Volley (association football)|volley]] by [[Cafu]] was saved by Leighton but rebounded off Scotland's [[Tom Boyd (Scottish footballer)|Tom Boyd]] for an [[Own goal (association football)|own goal]]. Scotland appealed for a second penalty in the final minute, claiming that [[Dunga]] had handled the ball, but it was not given and Brazil won 2–1.<ref name="GuardianBrazilScot"/> Their second game was against Morocco, on 16 June at the [[Stade de la Beaujoire]] in [[Nantes]]. [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]] gave Brazil the lead with his first World Cup goal nine minutes into the game, with [[Rivaldo]] and Bebeto also scoring to give Brazil a 3–0 win.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024354/ |at=Sport section, p.&nbsp;2 |work=[[The Guardian]] |title=Brazil in carnival mood |first=David |last=Lacey |date=17 June 1998 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231325/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024354/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil were already confirmed as winners of the group before their third game, against Norway, but Zagallo nonetheless picked most of his first-choice team, leaving out only [[Aldair]], who had received a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] against Scotland and risked suspension should he receive another, and Sampaio, who was already suspended.<ref name="sfdjoi"/> Brazil took the lead through Bebeto on 77 minutes. Norway responded with two goals in the last ten minutes – [[Tore André Flo]] scoring the equaliser and then winning a penalty when he was fouled by [[Marcelo Gonçalves Costa Lopes|Gonçalves]]. [[Kjetil Rekdal]] scored from the penalty spot to give Norway a 2–1 win.<ref name="sfdjoi">{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024488/ |first=Richard |last=Williams |author-link=Richard Williams (journalist) |title=Flo fizzes as Norway gatecrash party |newspaper=The Guardian |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |at=Sport section, p.&nbsp;3 |date=24 June 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231324/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88024488/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Brazil's opponents in [[1998 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the round of 16]] were [[Chile national football team|Chile]]; the match was played on 27 June at the [[Parc des Princes]] in Paris.<ref name="11v11BrazilChile">{{Cite web |title=Brazil v Chile, 27 June 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-chile-27-june-1998-265632/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030114529/https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-chile-27-june-1998-265632/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil took the lead on 11 minutes when a Dunga [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] was met by Sampaio, who was [[Marking (association football)|unmarked]] and headed the ball past goalkeeper [[Nelson Tapia]]. Sampaio doubled Brazil's lead on 27 minutes when he shot into the corner of the net following a long-range [[Roberto Carlos]] free kick. Ronaldo added a third from a penalty in first-half [[injury time]], after he had been fouled by Tapia.<ref>{{cite web |work=[[Orlando Sentinel]] |title=Ronaldo scores twice as Brazil ices Chile, 4–1 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |page=C3 |date=28 June 1998 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88026336/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231324/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88026336/the-orlando-sentinel/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Marcelo Salas]] pulled a goal back for Chile in the second half, but Ronaldo then scored again to seal a 4–1 Brazilian win.<ref name="11v11BrazilChile"/> In the quarter-finals they played [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] at the Stade de la Beaujoire on 3 July.<ref name="11v11BrazilDenmark">{{Cite web |title=Brazil v Denmark, 3 July 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-denmark-03-july-1998-265641/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030140100/https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-denmark-03-july-1998-265641/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Denmark scored the first goal two minutes into the game through [[Martin Jørgensen]], with Bebeto equalising eight minutes later. Brazil then took the lead with a goal by Rivaldo on 27 minutes, completing a move involving Dunga and Ronaldo. [[Brian Laudrup]] levelled the scores five minutes into the second half, but Rivaldo scored again 30 minutes before the end to seal a 3–2 win for Brazil.<ref>{{cite web |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |title=It's the End for Laudrup, Denmark |page=C6 |date=4 July 1998 |first=Grahame L. |last=Jones |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88030629/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231326/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88030629/the-los-angeles-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
Brazil's semi-final was against the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] at the [[Stade Vélodrome]] in [[Marseille]].<ref name="11v11BrazilNetherlands">{{Cite web |title=Brazil v Netherlands, 07 July 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-netherlands-07-july-1998-265644/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030144052/https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-netherlands-07-july-1998-265644/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Ronaldo gave Brazil the lead shortly after half-time, but [[Patrick Kluivert]] equalised for the Netherlands three minutes before the end. The game went to [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]], with the [[golden goal]] rule in effect.{{efn|The golden goal rule stipulates that 30 minutes of extra time is to be played, but that the game would end immediately should either team score in that period, with the scoring team being declared the winners.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Golden goal to decide Irish tie |last=Lacey |first=David |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=18 November 1995 |page=24 |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=12 June 2022 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/103625672/the-guardian/ |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613171719/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/103625672/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}} There was no further scoring, however, and the game was settled by a penalty shoot-out.<ref name="BBCBrazilNetherlands">{{Cite web |title=Brazil win penalties to reach final |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=8 July 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/128479.stm |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231324/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/128479.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> The two sides took their kicks alternately with Brazil kicking first and the first five penalties were all scored, giving Brazil a 3–2 lead.<ref name="11v11BrazilNetherlands"/> Brazilian goalkeeper [[Cláudio Taffarel]] then saved penalties from [[Phillip Cocu]] and [[Ronald de Boer]] with Dunga scoring again for Brazil, which meant Brazil won the shoot-out 4–2 and progressed to the final.<ref name="11v11BrazilNetherlands"/><ref name="BBCBrazilNetherlands"/>

