France were drawn in [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group C|Group C]] at the finals, alongside [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]], [[Saudi Arabia national football team|Saudi Arabia]] and [[South Africa national soccer team|South Africa]].<ref name="RSSSF98"/> They began their campaign on 12 June against World Cup debutants South Africa at the Stade Vélodrome. In a match which [[Richard Williams (journalist)|Richard Williams]] of ''[[The Guardian]]'' said they "dominated throughout", France won 3–0 with a goal in the first half from [[Christophe Dugarry]], a 77th minute own goal by South Africa's [[Pierre Issa]] and a last-minute strike by [[Thierry Henry]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88032342/ |first=Richard |last=Williams |author-link=Richard Williams (journalist) |work=[[The Guardian]] |title=France strike it rich |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=31 |date=13 June 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231327/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88032342/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
Their second game was against Saudi Arabia at the Stade de France on 18 June. Henry gave France the lead in the first half, scoring from a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] by [[Bixente Lizarazu]], before France scored three goals in the final fifteen minutes through [[David Trezeguet]], Henry again and Lizarazu to complete a 4–0 win. Both sides had a player [[Ejection (sports)|sent off]] – [[Mohammed Al-Khilaiwi]] in the 18th minute for Saudi Arabia and Zidane for France on 70 minutes for a stamp on [[Fuad Anwar]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Ian |author-link=Ian Ross (journalist) |last=Ross |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88032841/ |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=19 June 1998 |title=French joy is marred by Zidane sending-off |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |at=Sport section, p.&nbsp;6 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231326/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88032841/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Zidane was suspended for the next two games.<ref name="LATimesFranceDenmark"/> With progression to the knockout stage assured, Jacquet made eight changes to his team for the final game against Denmark at the [[Stade Gerland]] in [[Lyon]] on 24 June. France took the lead on 13 minutes when [[Youri Djorkaeff]] converted a penalty after [[Jes Høgh]] fouled Trezeguet. Denmark then won a penalty shortly before half-time for a [[Vincent Candela]] foul on Jørgensen which was scored by [[Michael Laudrup]]. In the second half, [[Emmanuel Petit]] scored with a low shot into the corner of the Danish goal to seal a 2–1 victory and first place in the group.<ref name="LATimesFranceDenmark">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88034517/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |first=Grahame L. |date=25 June 1998 |last=Jones |title=France Escapes Without a Mark, 2–1 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |page=C5 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231435/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88034517/the-los-angeles-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
France's round-of-16 opponents were [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]], at the [[Stade Félix-Bollaert]] in [[Lens]] on 28 June. In a match labelled a "real stinker" by Williams, with France described as "extraordinarily inept", there were no goals during the 90 minutes of normal time and the first period of extra time. With six minutes remaining, France broke the deadlock, [[Laurent Blanc]] scoring the FIFA World Cup's first ever golden goal to seal a 1–0 win and a place in the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YeONAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA68 |page=68 |title=Football, Europe and the Press |year=2013 |first1=Liz |last1=Crolley |first2=David |last2=Hand |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-13526-222-8 |access-date=27 May 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613171753/https://books.google.com/books?id=YeONAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA68 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=29 June 1998 |title=France turn dross into gold |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88035106/ |first=Richard |last=Williams |author-link=Richard Williams (journalist) |page=20 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231325/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88035106/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> There, they met Italy, the losing finalists from 1994, at Stade de France on 3 July. There were no goals during normal time and no golden goal, so the game was decided by a penalty shoot-out.<ref>{{cite web |work=[[El Paso Times]] |date=4 July 1998 |title=Deja blue: Italy loses on shootout |first=Mike |last=Penner |author-link=Mike Penner |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=3C |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88036406/ |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231326/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88036406/el-paso-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref> With France taking the first penalty, both sides scored their openers, before goalkeepers [[Gianluca Pagliuca]] and [[Fabien Barthez]] saved a penalty each, from Lizarazu and [[Demetrio Albertini]] respectively. The next five were all scored to leave France 4–3 ahead with one Italian penalty remaining. [[Luigi Di Biagio]] struck the crossbar with his kick and France advanced to the semi-finals with a 4–3 shoot-out win.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88045439/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |title=France, Brazil advance to semifinals |first=Ed |last=Osmond |agency=[[Bloomberg News]] |work=[[The Morning Call]] |date=4 July 1998 |page=45 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231327/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88045439/the-morning-call/ |url-status=live }}</ref> They returned to the Stade de France on 8 July for a semi-final against [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]]. After a goalless first half, there were two goals in quick succession shortly after half-time as [[Davor Šuker]] gave Croatia the lead before [[Lilian Thuram]] equalised for France. Thuram then gave France the lead on 70 minutes with his second and last goal for France. Despite Blanc being sent off five minutes later for violent conduct towards [[Slaven Bilić]], France held on for a 2–1 win and a place in the final.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88046513/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=David |last=Lacey |title=France finally find a place in the sun |page=34 |date=9 July 1998 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231329/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88046513/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="11v11FranceCroatia">{{Cite web |title=France v Croatia, 08 July 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/france-v-croatia-08-july-1998-265645/ |archive-date=20 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020013834/https://www.11v11.com/matches/france-v-croatia-08-july-1998-265645/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Blanc's red card was the only one he received in his entire career, and meant that he was suspended for the final. Bilić's reaction implied that Blanc had struck him on the head in the incident, but video replays showed that the impact had been to his chest or chin, and the incident is often cited as one of the most controversial in the competition's history.<ref>{{cite news |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=36 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/103625236/the-sydney-morning-herald/ |title=The French resolution |first=Michael |date=10 July 1998 |work=[[Sydney Morning Herald]] |last=Cockerill |access-date=12 June 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613171736/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/103625236/the-sydney-morning-herald/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=It was Laurent Blanc's fault he missed the 1998 World Cup final – Croatia's Slaven Bilic faces hostile reception during France friendly |last=Bairner |first=Robin |work=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |date=29 March 2011 |access-date=26 May 2022 |url=https://www.goal.com/en/news/1717/editorial/2011/03/29/2415008/it-was-laurent-blancs-fault-he-missed-the-1998-world-cup |archive-date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526210220/https://www.goal.com/en/news/1717/editorial/2011/03/29/2415008/it-was-laurent-blancs-fault-he-missed-the-1998-world-cup |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
Brazil was considered the favourites by [[bookmaker]]s before the match, with odds of 4–6 for them to win the tournament, compared with 6–5 for France.<ref>{{cite web |work=[[Ottawa Citizen]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88052181/ |date=10 July 1998 |page=G6 |title=Brazil is the odds-on favourite |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231327/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88052181/the-ottawa-citizen/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

'''Match officials'''

[[Said Belqola]] of Morocco was selected to [[Referee (association football)|referee]] the final. An amateur referee who worked as a [[customs officer]] professionally, he had begun officiating in 1983 before being selected to referee international matches in 1993.<ref>{{cite web |work=[[The Leaf-Chronicle]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |page=12 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=10 July 1998 |title=Belqolaa named referee for title match |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88052685/ |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231335/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88052685/the-leaf-chronicle/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Prior to the 1998 World Cup he had refereed two matches each at the [[1996 African Cup of Nations|1996]] and [[1998 African Cup of Nations]], including [[1998 African Cup of Nations Final|the final]] of the latter tournament, between South Africa and [[Egypt national football team|Egypt]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Africa v Cameroon, 13 January 1996 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/south-africa-v-cameroon-13-january-1996-246192/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205359/https://www.11v11.com/matches/south-africa-v-cameroon-13-january-1996-246192/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Zaire v Liberia, 25 January 1996 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/zaire-v-liberia-25-january-1996-246213/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205358/https://www.11v11.com/matches/zaire-v-liberia-25-january-1996-246213/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Congo DR v Togo, 09 February 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/congo-dr-v-togo-09-february-1998-265123/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205357/https://www.11v11.com/matches/congo-dr-v-togo-09-february-1998-265123/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=South Africa v Egypt, 28 February 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/south-africa-v-egypt-28-february-1998-265240/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205400/https://www.11v11.com/matches/south-africa-v-egypt-28-february-1998-265240/ |url-status=live }}</ref> At the 1998 World Cup he had refereed the [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group H#Argentina vs Croatia|Argentina vs Croatia]] and [[1998 FIFA World Cup Group F#Germany vs United States|Germany vs United States]] group games.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Argentina v Croatia, 26 June 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/argentina-v-croatia-26-june-1998-265610/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205357/https://www.11v11.com/matches/argentina-v-croatia-26-june-1998-265610/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany v USA, 15 June 1998 |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/germany-v-usa-15-june-1998-265578/ |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205401/https://www.11v11.com/matches/germany-v-usa-15-june-1998-265578/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[assistant referee]]s were [[Mark Warren (referee)|Mark Warren]] of England and Achmat Salie of South Africa, while Saudi Arabia's [[Abdul Rahman Al-Zaid]] was the [[fourth official]].<ref name="Final1998MatchCenter">{{Cite web |title=1998 FIFA World Cup France: Brazil – France |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/1998france/match-center/8788 |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030205653/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/1998france/match-center/8788 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Team selection===
In what was later described by writers for [[BBC Sport]] as the "great World Cup final mystery",<ref>{{Cite web |title=The great World Cup final mystery |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=2 April 2002 |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/history/newsid_1749000/1749324.stm |archive-date=17 February 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110217052606/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/history/newsid_1749000/1749324.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Ronaldo was omitted from the official teamsheet which Zagallo presented to FIFA at 7:48&nbsp;p.m. local time (5:48&nbsp;p.m. [[UTC]]), 72 minutes before kick off, with [[Edmundo (footballer)|Edmundo]] named in his place. Reporters for the BBC and other media received the news shortly after 8&nbsp;p.m. and had not expected this development, with [[John Motson]] describing scenes of "absolute mayhem and chaos" in the commentary box. At 8:18&nbsp;p.m., however, Brazil submitted a modified teamsheet with Ronaldo's name reinstated.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup stunning moments: Ronaldo falters as France win |last=Steinberg |first=Jacob |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=8 May 2018 |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/may/08/world-cup-stunning-moments-25-ronaldo-france |archive-date=29 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929021532/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/may/08/world-cup-stunning-moments-25-ronaldo-france |url-status=live }}</ref> It was disclosed several years later that Ronaldo had suffered a [[Seizure|convulsive fit]] on the afternoon of the final, had lost consciousness and spent three hours in hospital. Following preliminary tests that yielded no abnormalities, he was discharged and decided shortly before kickoff that he still intended to play.<ref name="CNNMystery">{{Cite web |title=The mystery of the 1998 World Cup final, and why Brazil's star striker barely turned up |last=Riddell |first=Don |publisher=[[CNN]] |date=12 July 2020 |access-date=30 October 2021 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2020/07/12/football/ronaldo-brazil-world-cup-final-1998-cmd-spt-intl/index.html |archive-date=30 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030202330/https://edition.cnn.com/2020/07/12/football/ronaldo-brazil-world-cup-final-1998-cmd-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
Concerned by the condition of their teammate, who nonetheless insisted on playing, the Brazilian squad missed their scheduled pre-match warmup.<ref name="CNNMystery"/>

With the exception of Cafu, who had been suspended for Brazil's semi-final match and returned in place of [[Zé Carlos (footballer, born 1968)|Zé Carlos]], Brazil therefore named an unchanged side with Edmundo on the [[Substitute (association football)|substitutes' bench]].<ref name="11v11BrazilNetherlands"/><ref name="CNNMystery"/><ref name="11v11BrazilFrance">{{cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-france-12-july-1998-265647/ |publisher=AFS Enterprises |title=Brazil v France, 12 July 1998 |work=11v11 |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=29 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211029151101/https://www.11v11.com/matches/brazil-v-france-12-july-1998-265647/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Blanc was suspended following his semi-final [[Penalty card#Red card|red card]] and his place in the French team was taken by [[Frank Leboeuf]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88054544/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[Austin American-Statesman]] |page=C8 |date=12 July 1998 |title=Breaking down today's final |first=Mike |last=Penner |access-date=30 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231336/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88054544/austin-american-statesman/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Otherwise, France named an unchanged team.<ref name="11v11FranceCroatia"/><ref name="11v11BrazilFrance"/>

==Match==
===First half===
[[File:Ballon Finale Coupe du Monde 1998.JPG|thumb|An exhibit on the final at the [[Musée National du Sport]] at the [[Allianz Riviera]] in Nice, containing a jersey with Jacquet's name and the match ball.]]
France kicked off the match at 9&nbsp;p.m. local time, in front of an attendance of 80,000 and an estimated global television audience of 1.7&nbsp;billion.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 4:30–4:33</ref><ref name="RSSSF98"/><ref name="11v11BrazilFrance"/><ref>{{cite web |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88111996/ |work=[[The Guardian]] |at=Sport p.&nbsp;2 |title=Road to the final |date=13 July 1998 |access-date=31 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231336/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88111996/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88112289/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=13 July 1998 |page=1C |title=By the numbers |newspaper=[[Florida Today]] |access-date=31 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231336/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88112289/florida-today/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The weather at [[Charles de Gaulle Airport]], {{convert|17|km|mi|sigfig=2}} from the stadium,{{efn|Distance measured using Google Maps distance calculator, between Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport, coordinates 49.0096774°N, 2.5457773°E and Stade de France, coordinates 48.9238848°N, 2.3580294°E.<ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Paris+Charles+de+Gaulle+Airport/@49.0096774,2.5457773,17z |access-date=29 October 2021 |title=Haneda Airport}}</ref><ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.co.uk/maps/place/Stade+de+France/@48.9238848,2.3580294,17z |access-date=20 October 2021 |title=Nissan Stadium}}</ref>}} was recorded as fair at the time of [[Kick-off (association football)|kick-off]], with a temperature of {{convert|73|F|C|order=flip}} and 50% humidity.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mauregard, Val-d'Oise, France Weather History – July 12, 1998 |publisher=[[The Weather Company]] |date= |access-date=31 October 2021 |url=https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/LFPG/date/1998-7-12 |archive-date=31 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211031205115/https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/LFPG/date/1998-7-12 |url-status=live }}</ref> France had a chance to score in the opening minute, when the ball was passed upfield towards [[Stéphane Guivarc'h]], who attempted a [[bicycle kick]] which went over the cross bar and landed on the top of the Brazilian net.<ref name="Clockwatch">{{cite web |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0000445/19980713/440/0042?browse=true |title=Clockwatch: How the World Cup was won and lost |work=[[Aberdeen Evening Express]] |page=42 |date=13 July 1998 |via=[[British Newspaper Archive]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=31 October 2021 }}</ref> Guivarc'h had another opportunity three minutes later, when he received a pass from Zidane on the edge of the penalty area and was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Taffarel, but his shot under pressure from [[Júnior Baiano]] was stopped by the goalkeeper.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 7:46–7:55</ref> On 5 minutes, Rivaldo hit a long cross to the far corner of the penalty area towards [[Leonardo Araújo|Leonardo]], who could not reach it; the ball went out for a [[goal kick]].<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 9:29–9:40</ref> France won a free kick a minute later when Cafu fouled Lizarazu, which Zidane curled into the penalty area. It reached Djorkaeff, who was unmarked; he attempted to score with a header, but it went high and wide of the goal.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 10:20–11:02</ref> On 17 minutes, Djorkaeff hit a long-range shot from the right-hand side, but it went wide of the Brazilian goal.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 21:12–11:02</ref> Five minutes later, Ronaldo received the ball near the [[touch-line]] and started running towards goal; from the left-hand edge of the penalty area he passed it to his right. Barthez almost tipped the ball into his own goal but was able to catch it before it crossed the line.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 25:49–26:02</ref> Brazil won a corner on 23 minutes, which was taken by Leonardo into the penalty area.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 27:12–27:25</ref> Sampaio directed a powerful header towards the goal, but it was aimed straight at Barthez who saved it.<ref name="Clockwatch"/>

Zidane gave France the lead just before the half-hour mark, outjumping Leonardo to connect with a header from an in-swinging corner from the right taken by Petit.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Your favourite World Cup moments: Zidane's glorious final double in Paris |last=Bairner |first=Robin |work=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |date=24 March 2014 |access-date=31 October 2021 |url=https://www.goal.com/en-sg/news/3868/main/2014/03/24/4705577/your-favourite-world-cup-moments-zidanes-glorious-final |archive-date=31 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211031164147/https://www.goal.com/en-sg/news/3868/main/2014/03/24/4705577/your-favourite-world-cup-moments-zidanes-glorious-final |url-status=live }}</ref> Four minutes later, Ronaldo received the ball following a long pass from Dunga, but Barthez punched his shot clear. Ronaldo and Barthez collided with each other during the incident; both needed assistance from medics but were able to continue the game.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 34:30–35:57</ref> Baiano received the first yellow card of the match on 33 minutes for a foul on Djorkaeff.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 37:14–37:30</ref> French captain [[Didier Deschamps]] was then shown a yellow card four minutes later for a diving challenge on Rivaldo.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 41:22–41:50</ref> On 41 minutes, Petit had a chance to score from {{convert|12|yards|m|sigfig=2}} when a pass forward by [[Christian Karembeu]] rebounded to him in space after hitting the back of Baiano's feet. Baiano recovered, however, to deflect the Frenchman's shot wide of the goalpost.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 44:53–45:20</ref> Shortly before first-half injury time, Thuram sent the ball upfield from deep within his own half, which was missed by the Brazilian defenders and reached Guivarc'h, one-on-one with Taffarel. The goalkeeper blocked his shot behind for a corner, however.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 48:34–49:10</ref> France won another corner a minute later, which was taken on the left by Djorkaeff into the penalty area, where Zidane once again headed the ball into the goal, through the legs of Roberto Carlos, to give France a 2–0 half-time lead.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 49:35–50:15</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88101217/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=13 July 1998 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |page=C8 |first=Helene |last=Elliott |title=Soul of French Team Finds Rare Air |access-date=31 October 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231336/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/88101217/the-los-angeles-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Second half===
The second half began with a Brazil substitution as [[Denílson (footballer, born 1977)|Denílson]] replaced Leonardo.<ref>{{cite web |date=14 July 1998 |title=Deschamps and Zidane Get Top Grades for Victorious France |url-access=subscription |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/07/14/sports/IHT-deschamps-and-zidane-get-top-grades-for-victorious-france.html |first=Peter |last=Berlin |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=31 October 2021 |archive-date=31 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211031182204/https://www.nytimes.com/1998/07/14/sports/IHT-deschamps-and-zidane-get-top-grades-for-victorious-france.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Marcel Desailly]] received a yellow card in the 49th minute for dissent, after the referee had penalised him for a foul on Cafu.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 57:40–58:00</ref><ref name="GuardianBrazilFranceFinal"/> On 51 minutes, Bebeto took a corner from the right-hand side which reached Denílson, who fell down as his attempted shot went wide of the goal while Deschamps was also challenging for the ball. Brazil's players appealed for a penalty, but Belqola did not award it.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:02:05–1:02:25</ref> A minute later, Dunga had a shot from outside the penalty area which went wide of Barthez's goal.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:03:00–1:03:15</ref> Karembeu was shown a yellow card on 55 minutes for a foul from behind on Cafu.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:05:59–1:06:17</ref> Rivaldo took the resulting free-kick short towards Roberto Carlos, who ran to the left edge of the penalty area before crossing it in, where it reached Ronaldo.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:06:17–1:06:53</ref> He shot from close range but Barthez saved.<ref name="Clockwatch"/> On 57 minutes, France made a change when [[Alain Boghossian]] came on in place of Karembeu.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:07:45–1:08:12</ref> Three minutes later, Roberto Carlos took a long [[throw-in]] from the left-hand side, which Barthez failed to catch near the edge of his penalty area, allowing Bebeto to take a shot, but Desailly blocked it.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:11:00–1:11:10</ref>

On 63 minutes, Guivarc'h was one-on-one with Taffarel following what FIFA commentators described as a "mistake" by the Brazilian defence, but he fired his shot wide of the goal.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:07:43–1:06:17</ref> Three minutes later Guivarc'h	was taken off, as Jacquet brought on Dugarry in his place.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:16:20–1:16:50</ref> On 68 minutes, Desailly made a sliding tackle on Cafu which was given as a foul by the referee. Belqola showed him a second yellow card, which reduced France to 10 men for the last 20 minutes. Desailly was just the third player to be sent off in a World Cup final after Argentina's [[Pedro Monzón]] and [[Gustavo Dezotti]], both in [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|1990]] in Rome against West Germany.<ref name="Clockwatch"/><ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:18:15–1:19:05</ref> 

After 74 minutes, Edmundo was brought on by Zagallo, replacing César Sampaio.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:24:50–1:25:05</ref> A minute later, France made a defensive substitution, bringing on [[Patrick Vieira]] in place of Djorkaeff.<ref name="Clockwatch"/> On 82 minutes, Dugarry was through on goal with only Taffarel to beat, but his shot went wide of the goal.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:33:07–1:33:20</ref> Brazil had a chance to score in second-half injury time when Denílson's shot hit the crossbar.<ref>{{harvnb|Brazil v France – 1998 FIFA World Cup Final – Full Match}}, 1:41:20–1:41:30</ref> Two minutes later, France created a final chance on the counterattack when Dugarry cleared Denílson's corner kick and found Vieira open on the left. Vieira then put his [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] teammate Petit through on goal with a first-touch through ball, and Petit finished low to Taffarel's left.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.soccertimes.com/worldcup/1998/games/jul12.htm|title=France plays perfect host; hoists World Cup in Paris.|date=12 July 1998|work=Soccer Times|access-date=24 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111128230026/http://www.soccertimes.com/worldcup/1998/games/jul12.htm|archive-date=28 November 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> Petit's goal was the 1000th in France national team history<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fff.fr/article/10619-djorkaeff-des-le-couloir-on-faisait-deux-tetes-de-plus-.html|date=12 July 2023|access-date=12 July 2023|language=FR|publisher=Fédération Française de Football|title=Djorkaeff: «Dès le couloir, on faisait deux têtes de plus...»|archive-date=12 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230712230729/https://www.fff.fr/article/10619-djorkaeff-des-le-couloir-on-faisait-deux-tetes-de-plus-.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and was the final kick of the game, sealing a 3–0 victory.<ref name="GuardianBrazilFranceFinal"/>

