==Post-match==
===France===
{{Quote box |quoted=true |bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |salign=center
|width=100%
|align=center
|quote={{lang|fr|Après avoir vu ça, on peut mourir tranquille. Enfin, le plus tard possible, mais on peut. Ah c'est superbe. Quel pied, ah quel pied! Oh putain!}}<br>After seeing this, one can die in peace. Though, hopefully, not too soon...This is great, fuck, this is the bollocks!
|source=[[Thierry Roland]] on [[TF1]]'s broadcast of the final after full time<ref>{{cite web|language=FR|title=Thierry Roland: «Après avoir vu ça, on peut mourir tranquille»|trans-title=Thierry Roland: "After seeing this, one can die in peace"|url=http://www.leparisien.fr/espace-premium/fait-du-jour/-17-06-2012-2052448.php|publisher=[[Le Parisien]]|date=17 June 2012|access-date=14 June 2019}}</ref> (translation by ''[[The Guardian]]''<ref>{{cite web |last1=Quigagne |first1=Kevin |title=Thierry Roland was France's David Coleman – with a hint of Roger Mellie |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/jun/23/thierry-roland-france-tv-commentator |website=The Observer |publisher=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=1 July 2024 |date=23 June 2012}}</ref>)
}}

France's win was their first World Cup title, becoming the seventh out of – {{as of|2025|lc=y}} – eight different countries to win the tournament. They also became the sixth team to win the competition as hosts and the first since [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] in [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]].

[[File:Tribune présidentielle finale France Brésil football 12 juillet 1998.jpg|thumb|right|French president [[Jacques Chirac]], the International Olympic Committee president [[Juan Antonio Samaranch]], the newly elected [[List of presidents of FIFA|FIFA president]] [[Sepp Blatter]] and his outgoing predecessor [[João Havelange]], UEFA president [[Lennart Johansson]], and co-president of local organizing committee [[Michel Platini]] were among those present at the stands during the awards ceremony. President Chirac handed the trophy to French captain Deschamps.<ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8R7ojiXL2VA |title=Matchday Live – 1998 Brazil vs. France |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=20 April 2018 |type=YouTube video |access-date=2 April 2020 |archive-date=6 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210106161209/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8R7ojiXL2VA |url-status=live}}</ref>]]
Millions of French supporters celebrated their team's victory in Paris throughout the night, with an estimated 1.5 million on the [[Champs-Élysées]] alone as players' names and faces along with celebratory messages were projected onto the [[Arc de Triomphe]]. It was reported that such scenes had not been seen there since Paris' [[Liberation of Paris|liberation]] from [[Nazi Germany|Nazi German]] occupation in late August 1944 during [[World War II]], including [[Charles de Gaulle]]'s parade down the same avenue on 26 August.<ref>{{cite AV media |title=France 98 : Nuit de fête sur les Champs-Elysées après la victoire (Archive INA) |trans-title=France 98: Night of celebration on the Champs-Elysées after the victory (INA Archive) |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WyuVIRDtel4 |publisher=Institut National de l'Audiovisuel |date=13 July 1998 |access-date=20 July 2023 |type=News broadcast |language=FR |via=[[YouTube]] |archive-date=20 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720151350/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WyuVIRDtel4 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

The next day, the Champs-Elysées was also packed with fans as the French players made an [[open-top bus]] tour down the avenue.<ref>{{Cite web |work=[[Kenosha News]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=14 July 1998 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100235961/kenosha-news/ |title=France savers World Cup glory |access-date=22 April 2022 |archive-date=22 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422093218/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100235961/kenosha-news/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The celebrations continued through to Tuesday, France's national [[Bastille Day]] holiday, with Chirac inviting the team to a garden party at the [[Élysée Palace]] following the [[Bastille Day military parade]] and praising the nation's solidarity during a speech there.<ref>{{Cite web |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |agency=[[Reuters]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=15 July 1998 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100236008/ |title=Bastille Day, soccer fame keep French in unity mood |access-date=22 April 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613231342/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100236008/chicago-tribune/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XkhLCnV0D48 |type=News broadcast |publisher=Institut National de l'Audiovisuel |access-date=8 December 2022 |date=5 May 2012 |title=France 98: La Garden Party du 14 juillet en intégralité (Archive INA) |trans-title=France 98: The 14th of July Garden Party in its entirety (INA Archive) |language=FR |via=[[YouTube]] |archive-date=28 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230828040029/https://www.youtube.com/watch?app=desktop&v=XkhLCnV0D48 |url-status=live }}</ref> The day after the final, ''[[L'Équipe]]'' led its front page with the banner headline ''Pour L'Éternité'' (For Eternity), with that 13 July edition becoming and remaining the most-sold edition in the newspaper's history with over 1.6 million copies sold.<ref>{{Cite web|language=FR|first=Émile|last=Gillet|title=Top 10 des meilleures ventes du journal L'Équipe : un seul ''intrus'' au milieu de l'Équipe de France|trans-title=Top 10 best-sellers of the newspaper ''L’Équipe'': Only one interloper in the middle of the France national football team|url=https://www.sportbuzzbusiness.fr/top-10-des-meilleures-ventes-du-journal-lequipe-un-seul-intrus-au-milieu-de-lequipe-de-france.html|publisher=SportBuzzBusiness.fr|date=27 June 2018|access-date=13 August 2021|archive-date=12 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230712224646/https://www.sportbuzzbusiness.fr/top-10-des-meilleures-ventes-du-journal-lequipe-un-seul-intrus-au-milieu-de-lequipe-de-france.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 24 July, each player was made a {{lang|FR|Chevalier}} (Knight) in France's [[Legion of Honour]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Décret du 24 juillet 1998 portant nomination à titre exceptionnel|language=fr|trans-title=Decree of 24 July 1998 on nomination to exceptional title|url=https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/jorf/id/JORFTEXT000000374701|website=Légifrance|access-date=1 July 2024|date=24 July 1998|archive-date=28 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210428230714/https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/jorf/id/JORFTEXT000000374701|url-status=live}}</ref>

An {{lang|FR|effet Mondial}} (World Cup effect) of France's victory on the country was cited, most notably economically.<ref>{{cite book|first1=Bastien|last1=Drut|first2=Richard|last2=Duhautois|first3=Simon|last3=Kupper|title=Sciences sociales football club|publisher=De Boeck Supérieur|trans-title=Football club social sciences|date=25 April 2017|page=103|ISBN=9782807313149|language=FR}}</ref> A [[political cartoon]] by [[Plantu]] published in ''[[L'Express]]'' after the final illustrated the issues that a multicultural French team winning the World Cup had posed to [[History of far-right movements in France|the French far-right]], reading: ''{{lang|FR|Pendant que l'équipe de France black-blanc-beur chante la Marseillaise et que le peuple français acclame Thuram, Zidane et Karembeu, un collaborateur console [[Jean-Marie Le Pen|(Jean-Marie) Le Pen]] en disant: "Ne pleure pas, Jean-Marie! Si ça se trouve, la finale du prochain Mondial, ce sera peut-être Nigeria-Cameroun," sur quoi Le Pen, tombe de sa chaise à la renverse.}}'' (As the black, white and Arabic French team sings ''La Marseillaise'' and as the French people acclaim Thuram, Zidane and Karambeu, a collaborator consoles Le Pen by saying, "Don't cry, Jean-Marie! It could be that the final of the next World Cup may be Nigeria vs. Cameroon," at which Le Pen falls out of his chair backwards.)<ref>{{cite book|first1=Hugh|last1=Dauncey|first2=Geoffrey|last2=Hare|title=France and the 1998 World Cup: The National Impact of a World Sporting Event|publisher=Routledge|date=February 28, 1999|ISBN=0714644382}}</ref>

The French players had made "[[I Will Survive]]" by [[Gloria Gaynor]] their unofficial anthem, particularly focusing on the instrumental sub-theme to sing "la la la la la" inspired by a remix of the song by [[Hermes House Band]]. Following France's victory, the song enjoyed immense popular enthusiasm amongst younger generations in the country 20 years after its original release in 1978. Gaynor also later released a version with the "la la la la la" chant included, called "I Will Survive '98".<ref name=chant>{{cite web |url=https://frenchly.us/french-sing-will-survive-soccer-games/ |title=Why Do the French Sing "I Will Survive" at Soccer Games? |first=Jenny |last=Hughes |date=12 July 2018 |work=Frenchly |access-date=30 October 2024 |archive-date=8 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208084758/https://frenchly.us/french-sing-will-survive-soccer-games/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2018, [[France 2]] aired a documentary in honor of the 20th anniversary of the final titled {{lang|FR|12 juillet 1998, le jour parfait}} (12 July 1998, The Perfect Day) with Jean-Pierre Devillers as director and Valérie Amarou as writer.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rousseau |first1=Christine |title=«12 juillet 1998, le jour parfait» : 20 ans après, France 2 revient sur la plus grande victoire des Bleus |trans-title="12 July 1998, The Perfect Day": 20 years after, France 2 remembers the Blues' biggest victory |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/televisions-radio/article/2018/06/05/tv-12-juillet-1998-le-jour-parfait_5310037_1655027.html |work=Le Monde |access-date=5 August 2024 |date=5 June 2018 |language=FR |archive-date=22 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250122122402/https://www.lemonde.fr/televisions-radio/article/2018/06/05/tv-12-juillet-1998-le-jour-parfait_5310037_1655027.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Devillers |first1=Jean-Pierre |title=12 juillet 1998, Le jour parfait |url=https://www.imdb.com/title/tt11851794/ |website=IMDb |access-date=5 August 2024 |date=6 May 2018 |archive-date=24 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250124081413/https://www.imdb.com/title/tt11851794/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite AV media |title=12 juillet 1998, le jour parfait |trans-title=12 July 1998, The Perfect Day |lang=FR |publisher=France 2 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oBXQSjaz104 |type=Documentary film |date=6 May 2018 |via=[[YouTube]] |access-date=5 August 2024 |archive-date=5 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240805152556/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oBXQSjaz104 |url-status=live }}</ref> Among the celebrities and fans who shared their experiences in the documentary were athletes who cited the victory as a catalyst, such as footballer [[Laura Georges]], épeé [[Fencing|fencer]] [[Laura Flessel-Colovic]], and [[Judo#Judoka (practitioner)|judoka]] [[Teddy Riner]].

===Brazil===
It was only the second time Brazil had lost a World Cup final, with the first being their [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|2–1 upset loss]] to [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] at the [[Maracanã Stadium]] in Rio de Janeiro in the final match of 1950 known as the ''Maracanaço'' (similarly, this marked Brazil's first World Cup final loss in a knockout format).<ref name="Britanica"/> The 3–0 scoreline was also Brazil's largest loss in the World Cup until their [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)|7–1 defeat]] to [[Germany national football team|Germany]] as hosts in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]] semi-final at the [[Mineirão]] in [[Belo Horizonte]], subsequently dubbed the ''Mineiraço''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brazil 1–7 Germany |last=McNulty |first=Phil |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=8 July 2014 |access-date=31 October 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28102403 |archive-date=12 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612022005/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28102403 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Ronaldo's fitness for the match and his initial omission and later reinstatement on Brazil's teamsheet became a subject of ongoing journalistic interest following the match, with analysis continuing more than 20 years later.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The mystery of the 1998 World Cup final |last=Riddell |first=Don |publisher=[[CNN]] |date=12 July 2020 |access-date=22 April 2022 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2020/07/12/football/ronaldo-brazil-world-cup-final-1998-cmd-spt-intl/index.html |archive-date=27 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527195602/https://edition.cnn.com/2020/07/12/football/ronaldo-brazil-world-cup-final-1998-cmd-spt-intl/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> A number of [[conspiracy theory|conspiracy theories]] regarding the incident emerged, including an allegation by striker Edmundo (who had been set to play in Ronaldo's place) that Brazil's team sponsor [[Nike, Inc.|Nike]] had pressured Zagallo to reinstate the player.<ref name="RonaldoFourFourTwo">{{Cite web |title=What Really happened to Ronaldo before the 1998 World Cup Final – in his own words |last=De Campos Jr. |first=Celso |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |publisher=[[Future plc|Future]] |date=20 March 2020 |access-date=22 April 2022 |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/what-really-happened-ronaldo-1998-world-cup-final-his-own-words |archive-date=14 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220414202417/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/what-really-happened-ronaldo-1998-world-cup-final-his-own-words |url-status=live }}</ref> Zagallo also said that the decision was his and the player's, saying "if there had been interference, I would have resigned".<ref name="GraunRonaldo">{{Cite web |title=World Cup stunning moments: Ronaldo falters as France win |last=Steinberg |first=Jacob |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=8 May 2018 |access-date=22 April 2022 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/may/08/world-cup-stunning-moments-25-ronaldo-france |archive-date=29 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929021532/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/may/08/world-cup-stunning-moments-25-ronaldo-france |url-status=live }}</ref> A government inquiry into Nike's involvement with Brazil was launched by politician [[Aldo Rebelo]] and interviewed numerous players, officials and team doctors, but it cleared Nike of any wrongdoing.<ref>{{Cite web |title=How Nike bought Brazil |last=Bellos |first=Alex |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=9 July 2001 |access-date=22 April 2022 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2001/jul/09/marketingandpr.worldcupfootball2002 |quote=Aldo Rebelo |archive-date=22 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422091433/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2001/jul/09/marketingandpr.worldcupfootball2002 |url-status=live }}</ref> Another theory, promulgated in Brazilian newspaper ''[[Folha de S.Paulo]]'', was that Ronaldo had a [[nervous breakdown]] during the tournament, and some players also said that team doctor Lidio Toledo had cried about Ronaldo's condition when he encountered him in the dressing room.<ref name="RonaldoFourFourTwo"/> Toledo did not sign him off as unfit however, later saying "imagine if I stopped him playing and Brazil lost, at that moment I'd have to go and live on the North Pole."<ref name="GraunRonaldo"/>

===Subsequent tournaments===
France followed up their victory by [[UEFA Euro 2000 qualification|qualifying]] for and [[UEFA Euro 2000 Final|winning]] [[UEFA Euro 2000]] held in the Netherlands and Belgium.<ref>{{cite news |title=France win Euro 2000 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/816194.stm |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=2 July 2000 |access-date=12 June 2011 |archive-date=29 April 2004 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040429010001/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/816194.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> This made them just the second country to be world and European champions simultaneously after [[West Germany national football team|West Germany]] won [[UEFA Euro 1972|Euro 1972]] and then the [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974 World Cup]] as hosts. Only [[Spain national football team|Spain]] has since duplicated the feat after winning [[UEFA Euro 2008|Euro 2008]], the [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010 World Cup]] and [[UEFA Euro 2012|Euro 2012]].

Brazil took the [[Copa América]] title in [[1999 Copa América|1999]] and then won the [[2002 FIFA World Cup|next World Cup]] in Japan and South Korea in 2002 for their record fifth World Cup win as Ronaldo scored both goals in [[2002 FIFA World Cup final|the final]] against Germany in Yokohama.<ref>{{cite news |first=Brian |last=Homewood |title=Football: Rivaldo's rousing finale |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-rivaldos-rousing-finale-1107387.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-rivaldos-rousing-finale-1107387.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=[[The Independent]] |date=19 July 1999 |access-date=12 June 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Brazil crowned world champions |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2067000/2067939.stm |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=30 June 2002 |access-date=12 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110511131526/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/matches_wallchart/germany_v_brazil/newsid_2067000/2067939.stm |archive-date=11 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> France were eliminated in the group stage at the 2002 tournament, becoming the first World Cup holders to be eliminated without reaching the knockout stage since [[1966 FIFA World Cup|Brazil in 1966]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany struck by the curse of the World Cup winner |first=Peter |last=Rutzler |work=[[i (British newspaper)|i]] |date=27 June 2018 |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://inews.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/germany-be-struck-by-the-curse-of-the-world-cup-winner-166182 |archive-date=24 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210924165229/https://inews.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/germany-be-struck-by-the-curse-of-the-world-cup-winner-166182 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

The two countries' next World Cup meeting was in a quarter-final in Stuttgart of the subsequent tournament in [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]], where a goal from Henry off a Zidane free kick was the match's only goal. France went on to return to the [[2006 FIFA World Cup final|final]], where they lost to Italy on penalties. They were eliminated at the group stage again in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]] before winning their second World Cup [[2018 FIFA World Cup|in 2018]] with Deschamps as coach.<ref name="Britanica"/>

